The scorecards `make eval` writes land here, one file per day it was run:
`<date>.tsv`, one header row and one row per case. A second run on the same day
appends to that day's file rather than replacing it, and its rows are told apart
by when the run started and by the prompt's fingerprint.

They are committed, results and all. They hold counts and dollars — no prompt
text and no model output — so a scorecard can neither leak a key nor embarrass
anybody, and two of them side by side are how a prompt change is judged.
